          "flightIncluded": false,
          "flights_included": false,
          "location": "Private Trip",
          "routeSummary": "",
          "overviewText": "This Meghalaya Trip is one's for people who don't want to choose between hills and wildlife.\n\nMost Meghalaya trips end at Dawki or Mawlynnong. We stretch this one two days further, out of the hills entirely, and into Kaziranga for a jeep safari. Seven days. One small group. Two very different landscapes, back to back.\n\nThe first half runs the classic Meghalaya route. Shillong to start: pine forests, hill markets, a slow warm-up into the trip. Laitlum Canyon on day two, where the valley just falls away below you and the clouds move through it like they're in a hurry. Then Cherrapunji, wet enough to feel it in the air the moment you arrive.\n\nDay three is the trek, down to the Double Decker Living Root Bridge, then out to Seven Sisters Falls. By day four and five, the pace drops. Dawki's river, still enough to look painted on. Mawlynnong's bamboo walkways. A last waterfall stop at Krang Suri before the trip turns.\n\nThat's where day six comes in. We drive you straight out of the hills and into Kaziranga's grassland, a different world entirely. Day seven, you're up before sunrise for a jeep safari, tracking rhinos through the morning mist, before we get you to Guwahati airport.\n\nNobody leaves this one saying they wish they'd added something.\n\n\n### 📍 Where This Meghalaya Trip Takes You\n\n1. **Shillong:** Your first two nights. Pine-covered hills, a capital that doesn't feel like one, markets and cafes open well into the evening. Easiest place on the route to settle into the trip's pace.\n2. **Laitlum Canyon:** \"End of the hills\" in Khasi, and the view backs that up. The land drops off sharply into a valley that stays half-covered in cloud most of the day. We time this one for golden hour.\n3. **Cherrapunji \\(Sohra\\):** One of the wettest spots on the planet, and it shows in how fast the weather turns here. Waterfalls, limestone caves, and root bridge country all sit within a short drive of each other.\n4. **Double Decker Living Root Bridge:** Two tiers, grown from rubber tree roots over generations, still used daily by locals. Getting there means a proper trek — steps, streams, dense forest the whole way down.\n5. **Seven Sisters Falls:** Seven streams dropping together off the same cliff face. Heaviest right after monsoon. Viewed from across the valley, so no trek required for this one.\n6. **Mawsmai Cave:** A short limestone cave walk, 20–30 minutes, no gear needed. A breather between the bigger days.\n7. **Mawlynnong:** Asia's cleanest village — bamboo-paved paths, a living root bridge on the village edge, and a bamboo skywalk with a view that stretches into Bangladesh on a clear day.\n8. **Dawki:** The Umngot river's home turf. The water's clear enough that boats look like they're hovering in photos. This is your base for the water activities.\n9. **Krang Suri / Phe Phe Falls:** A turquoise pool, off the usual tourist track, calm enough to swim in. The last stop before the trip turns toward Kaziranga.\n10. **Kaziranga National Park:** A UNESCO-listed grassland and the world's biggest population of one-horned rhinos. We get you in on a jeep at first light, when the animals are most active and the mist hasn't lifted yet.\n\n### ✅ Things To Do In Meghalaya On This Trip\n\n✓ Trekking down to the Double Decker Living Root Bridge through Cherrapunji's forest\n\n✓ Cliff jumping into the Umngot river at Dawki\n\n✓ Snorkelling in water clear enough to see straight to the riverbed\n\n✓ Boating across Dawki's glass-still river\n\n✓ Walking through Mawsmai Cave's limestone passages\n\n✓ A bonfire by the river at Dawki, weather permitting\n\n✓ Catching Laitlum Canyon at sunrise or sunset\n\n✓ A dawn jeep safari through Kaziranga, tracking rhinos, elephants, and swamp deer\n\n### Why This Trip Over the Standard Meghalaya Route\n\n- **It doesn't stop at the hills:** You get the full Meghalaya circuit and a Kaziranga safari without booking two separate trips.\n- **Nothing's left out:** Root bridges, waterfalls, caves, the Umngot river, a safari — one itinerary covers all of it.\n- **You're not doing this alone:** Small group format, built-in company, shared costs, none of the usual solo-trip logistics.\n- **We run the whole thing:** Stays, transport, permits, safari bookings, a trip captain on call throughout. You just need to show up.\n- **The pace is built to not burn you out:** Trekking days are followed by slower ones, and Kaziranga's flat grassland at the end is a genuine change of scenery, not more of the same.\n\n**Start planning your Meghalaya Trip NOW!**",
